english1:
Create an 8-second, vertical 9:16, photorealistic live-action cinematic short film set during a zombie apocalypse.

CORE THEME: LOVE CONQUERS THE INFECTION AND SAVES THE PERSON YOU LOVE.

The story must have a clear emotional progression: the healthy boyfriend searches with his flashlight → its beam reveals his infected girlfriend → she tilts her head and slowly raises her hands → he recognizes her and cries → he drops the flashlight → he rushes toward her and chooses to embrace her → her infection fades away → they reunite happily in a golden field.

CHARACTER IDENTITY AND REFERENCE IMAGE MAPPING

Reference Image 1 is the WOMAN, and she is the infected girlfriend.

Reference Image 2 is the MAN, and he is the healthy boyfriend who saves her.

Strictly preserve both characters' recognizable facial identities, facial features, face shapes, eye shapes, noses, lips, hairstyles, hair colors, hair lengths, and natural body proportions. They must remain the exact same two people throughout every shot.

The infection may change the woman's temporary skin tone, eye appearance, head posture, and body movements. It must not change her underlying facial identity.

The man remains healthy throughout the entire video. Do not swap their identities, genders, infection status, faces, hairstyles, or roles.

STRICT OUTFIT, JEWELRY, AND ACCESSORY CONSISTENCY

Use each uploaded reference image as the primary source of truth for that character's appearance and styling. Preserve the original outfit as accurately as possible across every shot.

For both characters, retain all visible clothing details, including garment type, color, silhouette, fabric, texture, neckline, sleeves, patterns, prints, buttons, zippers, and distinctive design elements.

Preserve all visible jewelry and accessories, including earrings, necklaces, rings, bracelets, watches, glasses, hair accessories, hats, scarves, and other personal items. Keep their original shapes, colors, materials, sizes, and positions consistent with the reference images.

Do not replace the woman's original clothes with a generic white sweater or the man's clothes with a generic black T-shirt or jacket. Do not invent new clothing, jewelry, or accessories. Do not exchange clothing or accessories between the characters.

The infected woman may have subtle dust, dirt, and minor environmental wear on her original clothes, but her original outfit must remain recognizable. Do not substantially change the garment's color, design, or structure.

When the infection fades, restore her natural skin tone while retaining the same face, hairstyle, original outfit, jewelry, and accessories. In the final golden-field scene, both characters must retain the same outfits and visible accessories from their reference images.

If an item is hidden or cropped out of a reference image, do not invent distinctive details that cannot be seen.

SHOT 1 — 0–2 SECONDS: HE SEARCHES WITH A FLASHLIGHT, THEN ITS BEAM REVEALS HER

0–0.8 seconds — OPEN ON THE HEALTHY MAN FROM REFERENCE IMAGE 2. Medium close-up or three-quarter view on a ruined street at night. His recognizable face and the flashlight in his hand must both be visible. He anxiously searches the thick blue-gray smoke, slowly sweeping the flashlight ahead of him. The infected woman is not yet clearly visible. Do not open on the woman with her arms already raised.

0.8–2 seconds — Follow his flashlight beam into the smoke and reveal the infected woman from Reference Image 1. The beam visibly illuminates her face before she moves. Her skin has a subtle gray infection tint, her eyes look hollow, and her head tilts slightly to one side. Her arms start lowered; only AFTER the light reveals her does she slowly raise both hands toward him, fingers slightly spread, with stiff, hesitant movements. Do not begin with a completed arms-out pose. She remains frightening but recognizable, with exactly her own original face, hairstyle, outfit, jewelry and accessories.

Maintain spatial continuity: the man holds the flashlight, its beam reaches the woman, and she faces him, not an unrelated camera. Cold blue-gray cinematic light, realistic smoke and drifting ash. The flashlight is the one required story prop; do not replace either person's reference clothing or accessories.

SHOT 2 — 2–3.5 SECONDS: HE RECOGNIZES HER, CRIES, DROPS THE FLASHLIGHT, AND RUSHES TOWARD HER

Cut back to a tight close-up of the same man from Reference Image 2, still holding the SAME flashlight he used in Shot 1. Looking directly at the woman just revealed by its beam, he recognizes his beloved girlfriend. His expression shifts from alert fear to shock, heartbreak and love; his eyes fill with tears and visible tears roll down his cheeks.

Only AFTER recognition and visible tears, his grip loosens. Briefly show the flashlight slipping out of his hand and falling naturally onto the dusty ground; its beam swings downward and remains on the ground. Do not drop it before he has illuminated and recognized her. Do not duplicate it, make it float, or let it vanish.

With his hands now empty, he immediately rushes the short distance toward her and opens his arms to initiate the embrace in Shot 3. This is a loving approach, not an attack, tackle or collision. Preserve his exact facial identity, hairstyle, original clothing, jewelry and accessories.

MANDATORY ACTION ORDER: the man searches while holding the flashlight → its beam reveals the woman → she tilts her head and slowly raises her hands → he recognizes her and visibly cries → he drops the flashlight → he rushes toward her to hug her. Show the flashlight search and reveal before the recognition reaction; do not skip straight to the infected woman reaching out.

SHOT 3 — 3.5–5.5 SECONDS: HE INITIATES THE EMBRACE AND LOVE BEGINS TO SAVE HER

The infected woman slowly raises both hands and moves toward the man with stiff, instinctive movements, as if she might lunge at him.

The man does not retreat. He deliberately steps toward her, opens both arms, and firmly embraces her without hesitation.

IMPORTANT: THE MAN MUST INITIATE THE HUG. He actively chooses to embrace her despite her infected appearance. She must not knock him down or attack him.

Their arms naturally wrap around each other's shoulders and backs. Their bodies make believable physical contact. The embrace feels intimate, emotionally powerful, and physically realistic, with correct anatomy and no body clipping.

Initially, the woman's body remains stiff. As he holds her tightly, her shoulders gradually relax and her body softens. The gray pallor on her face begins to fade naturally. Her skin returns toward its original healthy tone, and the emptiness in her eyes slowly gives way to warmth and recognition.

Show the infection fading through a subtle, continuous cinematic transformation, not a sudden face swap or a magical flash that changes her identity.

Her original clothing, hairstyle, earrings, necklace, rings, bracelets, watch, and other visible accessories must remain consistent throughout the transformation. The man's original appearance must also remain unchanged.

The camera smoothly pushes closer to their faces as they embrace. The emotional focus is grief, tenderness, sacrifice, and the decision not to abandon the person he loves.

SHOT 4 — 5.5–8 SECONDS: A GOLDEN-FIELD REUNION WITH INTIMATE FACIAL CLOSE-UPS

At the emotional peak of their embrace, create a cinematic match cut using the same hugging pose.

The cold gray smoke gradually transforms into warm golden sunset light. The ruined street transitions into a beautiful, peaceful golden field at golden hour.

The woman has now fully recovered and must remain exactly the same person as Reference Image 1. The man must remain exactly the same person as Reference Image 2.

Preserve both characters' original outfits, visible jewelry, accessories, hairstyles, hair colors, and facial identities. Do not change their clothes simply because the environment has become romantic.

MANDATORY CAMERA FRAMING:
Use a tight close-up or intimate close medium shot. Both faces must be large, sharp, clearly visible, and occupy most of the frame. Show their faces, shoulders, and upper bodies while they embrace. Their facial expressions must be the visual center of the final shot.

NO WIDE SHOTS. NO DISTANT FIGURES. NO BACK-ONLY SHOTS. Do not let either person's hair, shoulder, or head completely obscure the other's face.

The woman, now healthy, rests her face close to the man's. He gently strokes her hair and holds her tenderly. They briefly touch foreheads, then pull back just enough to look directly into each other's eyes and realize that she is safe.

Their expressions change into sincere, tender, happy smiles through their tears. Their eyes glisten with tears, their lips curve naturally, and their faces clearly communicate relief, affection, and the joy of being reunited.

Warm golden sunlight illuminates both faces. A gentle breeze moves the woman's hair and original clothing naturally. The background is softly blurred with golden grass and warm sunset bokeh.

The camera makes a subtle final push-in and holds on their intimate facial close-up as they embrace and smile at each other.

The final moment must clearly communicate the story's message: LOVE HAS SAVED HER. Love has overcome the infection, and the couple has found each other again.

VISUAL STYLE AND CINEMATOGRAPHY

Photorealistic live-action cinema, natural skin texture, realistic facial expressions, visible natural tears, physically believable human movement, anatomically correct hands, cinematic lighting, shallow depth of field, realistic volumetric smoke, subtle film grain, and smooth camera movement.

The first half uses cold, desaturated blue-gray tones to convey danger, fear, and despair. The second half transitions to warm amber and golden-hour sunlight to convey healing, hope, tenderness, and happiness.

Prioritize stable character identity, original clothing and accessories, clear action continuity, emotional close-ups, and readable facial expressions over complicated camera movements or excessive background detail.

STRICT NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS

No character identity changes.
No face swapping or changes to facial structure.
No gender swapping or infection-role swapping.
The woman is infected in the first half; the man remains healthy.
No replacement clothing, invented jewelry, disappearing earrings, changing necklaces, duplicated accessories, or randomly shifting jewelry.
No sudden outfit changes during the infection transformation or the golden-field transition.
No extra people or duplicate characters.
No deformed hands, extra fingers, extra limbs, or unnatural body proportions.
No body clipping, floating objects, or teleportation.
No flashlight floating in midair or disappearing before falling.
No man running away from the infected woman.
No woman knocking the man down.
No exaggerated grotesque monster transformation.
No distant final shot, no obscured faces, and no back-only ending.
No random mixing of the ruined city and golden field.
No cartoon, anime, or video-game CGI style.
No subtitles, text, logos, or watermarks.

